WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Recall the earlier factorization: n
equals two to the two s times w squared,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.020
with s at least one and w odd.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.160
The original parameter u equals
two to the s minus one times w.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.980
Assume, for contradiction, that s is
at least two. Under that assumption,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.560
the preceding descent would leave a cycle
of length M equal to two to the s plus

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.660
one, with odd coefficients b sub
j and scalar norm four w squared.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.240
Read the identity coefficient of
the final polynomial times its star.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.260
A term b sub j times X
to the j meets a starred

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.300
term b sub ell times
X to the negative ell.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.940
Their product returns to the
identity position exactly when

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.980
j minus ell is zero modulo M.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.380
With both indices between zero and M
minus one, this means j equals ell.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.920
Matching pairs therefore contribute
the squares b sub j squared.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:09.740
Collecting them gives a sum of M
odd squares equal to four w squared.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.080
Our eight position illustration
shows the smallest possible endpoint.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.580
Now reduce modulo eight. Write an
odd integer b as two r plus one.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.580
Its square is one plus four times the
product of the consecutive integers r

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.320
and r plus one. One of those integers
is even, so their product is even.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.730
The multiple of four is
therefore a multiple of eight.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.580
Every odd square has residue one.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.780
Replace each square on the left
by one. Since s is at least two,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.290
M equals eight times
two to the s minus two.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.700
Group the ones into blocks of
eight. Every block has residue zero,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.800
so the whole left side has residue
zero. But w is odd as well.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.040
Its square has residue one, so four w
squared has residue four modulo eight.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.460
The same integer equation would require
zero to equal four. That is impossible.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.660
The conditional descent
endpoint cannot exist.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.310
Therefore s cannot be at least two.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.060
We already had s at least
one, so s equals one.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.160
Substitute this into the original
u: two to the zero times w equals w,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.480
which is odd. The order becomes
n equals four u squared,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.900
with u odd. This completes
the order restriction.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.840
The case u equals one gives order
four and survives this argument.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.560
Its known circulant Hadamard matrix
remains an example. The remaining

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.840
proof will assume u greater than one
and exclude those larger candidates.
